export const puzzleOrigins: Readonly<Record<number, string>> = {
  1: 'The expression began as “heels over head,” a much older description of tumbling upside down. The order gradually flipped to “head over heels,” and by the 1800s it was also being used for falling deeply in love.',
  2: 'People have compared life to a repeating cycle for thousands of years, across many cultures and religions. The exact English phrase has no single known inventor; Disney’s The Lion King later made it especially familiar to modern audiences.',
  3: 'This comes from judged sports, especially boxing. When the judges do not all choose the same winner, their divided verdict is officially called a split decision.',
  4: 'The modern phrase grew from the nine-dot puzzle, whose solution requires drawing beyond an imagined square. It became popular in business and creativity writing during the 1960s and 1970s.',
  5: 'The expression became common in the 1800s. It treats written lines as carrying a second, unstated message in the spaces between them—something the reader must infer rather than read directly.',
  6: 'The heart has represented emotion since ancient times, so a “broken heart” became a natural image for grief and lost love. Versions of the metaphor appear in very old religious and literary writing.',
  7: 'This is an American expression. One early printed example appeared in the Galveston Daily News in 1881, describing people who seemed powerful only within their limited local surroundings.',
  8: 'This is mainly a literal combination rather than an old idiom with one traceable origin. A backwards glance simply means turning to look at what has been left behind.',
  9: 'Its precise origin is disputed. It entered widely used English through forms of pidgin speech in the 1800s and may reflect either Chinese Pidgin English or representations of Native American speech.',
  10: '“Man overboard” developed as a direct warning at sea: someone had fallen from a vessel into the water. Its short, urgent wording lets a crew react immediately.',
  11: '“Once in a blue moon” has meant “very rarely” since the 1800s. The saying is older than today’s popular definition of a blue moon as an extra full moon, so that calendar explanation is not its original source.',
  12: 'Crossroads have been important meeting places since ancient road networks. Because a traveller must choose a path there, the word naturally developed its figurative meaning: a moment of major choice or change.',
  13: 'The phrase became popular in the early 1900s as a humorous description of physical awkwardness, especially poor dancing. Imagining both feet as left feet neatly explains the lack of coordination.',
  14: '“Small talk” has been used since the 1700s for polite conversation about unimportant subjects. “Small” refers to the light weight of the topics, rather than the length of the conversation.',
  15: 'This is a literal instruction that became an everyday expression as organised queues became common. American English usually says “stand in line,” while British and Australian English more often uses “queue.”',
  16: 'The expression developed from several Middle English forms meaning “top turned downward.” Its spelling and word order shifted over centuries before settling into the modern “upside down.”',
  17: '“Nowhere” has described an unknown or remote place for centuries. Adding “the middle of” exaggerates the isolation, suggesting a place so distant that there is nothing recognisable in any direction.',
  18: 'Ideas about existence after death are ancient and appear in religions and philosophies around the world. The English expression is therefore descriptive, not a saying with one identifiable author.',
  19: '“Top secret” grew from official security classifications used by governments and armed forces in the early 1900s. “Top” marked the highest level of secrecy, above ordinary confidential material.',
  20: '“Three Blind Mice” is an old English nursery rhyme. A version of its words and music was printed in Thomas Ravenscroft’s Deuteromelia in 1609, though later verses changed over time.',
  21: 'The image comes from flowing water that has already passed beneath a bridge and cannot be brought back. Related versions existed earlier, but the familiar English wording became common in the 1900s.',
  22: 'English has long described the transition into sleep as “falling,” much like falling into silence or darkness. It is an old everyday metaphor rather than a phrase coined by one known person.',
  23: 'Stars have long symbolised fame and brilliance. Calling a promising newcomer a “rising star” extends the image: the person’s reputation appears to be climbing higher and becoming brighter.',
  24: '“Half-baked” first described food taken from an oven before it was ready. By the 1600s it was being applied figuratively to plans and ideas that had not been thought through or fully developed.',
  25: 'The expression grew from the ordinary duties expected during a working day. By the late 1800s it was being used to dismiss even an unusual task as routine: simply part of the day’s work.',
  26: 'Its exact source is still uncertain. The phrase appeared in print in the 1950s and probably draws on numbered games; popular stories linking it specifically to BBC football commentary are not supported by solid evidence.',
  27: 'This is a straightforward metaphor based on travelling toward a destination. A useful action may not finish the journey, but it can still be one step along the correct path.',
  28: 'Peas from the same pod are nearly identical and sit closely together. English writers were comparing very similar people or things to peas in a pod by the 1500s.',
  29: 'The phrase was recorded in the 1800s and originally covered several kinds of discomfort or low spirits. A seafaring explanation is often repeated, but the evidence is not strong enough to make its exact origin certain.',
  30: '“Between you and me” has long introduced information meant to remain private. Adding “just” strengthens the idea that the confidence should go no farther than the two people speaking.',
  31: '“Split personality” came from early psychological language in the late 1800s and early 1900s. Popular culture later broadened it into a loose description of someone who seems to have sharply contrasting sides.',
  32: 'A “double take” comes from stage and screen comedy. An actor first looks, then quickly looks again after realising that something surprising has just been seen.',
  33: 'A “cut” once commonly meant a person’s style, shape or quality. By the 1800s, being “a cut above” someone meant belonging to a noticeably better class or standard.',
  34: 'The phrase adds one impossible extra day to eternity for emphasis. Shakespeare used “for ever and a day” in The Taming of the Shrew, helping preserve a form already understood in his time.',
  35: '“First aid” became established in the late 1800s as organised training taught people to give immediate help before a doctor arrived. Early ambulance and voluntary medical organisations helped spread the term.',
  36: 'This is an old, literal way of saying that two people or things are next to one another. Its repeated structure gives equal weight to both sides, which also made it useful figuratively for close cooperation.',
  37: 'The expression borrows from sport: a level field gives neither side an uphill advantage. Its modern figurative use for fair conditions became especially common in business and politics in the late 1900s.',
  38: 'People have imagined a human face or figure in the Moon’s light and dark patches since ancient times. Different cultures tell different stories about the figure, so there is no single “man in the moon” legend.',
  39: 'A fast lane is literally the part of a multi-lane road used by quicker traffic. As motorways spread in the 1900s, it became a metaphor for an exciting, competitive or high-pressure lifestyle.',
  40: 'The phrase grew from journalism and publishing: an “inside story” promised facts known by people close to an event. “Inside” suggested privileged information unavailable to the general public.',
  41: 'A cornerstone was traditionally the first major stone set in a masonry foundation, guiding the position of the rest. That essential structural role produced its figurative meaning: a basic and indispensable part.',
  42: 'Height has long represented power, success and happiness. Being “on top of the world” exaggerates that image to its greatest possible height, describing someone who feels completely triumphant or joyful.',
  43: 'Earth has long symbolised what is solid, ordinary and real. “Down to earth” therefore came to describe someone practical and unpretentious rather than lofty, dreamy or removed from everyday life.',
  44: 'This is a direct timekeeping expression rather than an idiom with a single known inventor. To arrive “right on time” is to arrive at exactly the appointed moment.',
  45: 'This phrase developed naturally from schedules and deadlines. Someone or something “ahead of time” reaches a stage earlier than the time expected or arranged.',
  46: 'The expression comes from early phonograph records. When a damaged groove made the needle jump back and replay the same sound, the record literally repeated itself again and again.',
  47: 'This is a literal description rather than a historic idiom. Reading backwards can mean moving through letters or words in the reverse of their usual direction.',
  48: 'A turning point was originally a physical place or moment where movement changed direction. By the 1800s it was commonly being used for an event that changes the course of a life, argument or history.',
  49: 'The phrase became strongly associated with 20th-century aviation. Once an aircraft had used too much fuel to return safely, it had passed its point of no return; the idea then spread to everyday decisions.',
  50: '“Missing link” became famous during 19th-century debates about evolution. It referred to a hypothetical fossil form connecting known groups, then broadened to mean any absent piece needed to complete an explanation.',
  51: 'A chain reaction is a scientific process in which one event triggers the next, especially in chemistry and nuclear physics. During the 1900s it also became a general metaphor for a spreading sequence of consequences.',
  52: 'The high five became widely visible in American sport during the late 1970s. Several people have been credited with inventing it, so its precise first use remains disputed.',
  53: '“Profile” can mean the outline seen from the side. A low profile is less visible, and during the 1900s the phrase became a common way to describe avoiding attention or publicity.',
  54: 'People say “it’s a small world” after discovering an unexpected connection across distance. The expression predates Disney’s 1964 attraction and song, although they made the wording internationally famous.',
  55: 'The “big picture” is the complete view rather than one small detail. The figurative phrase became especially common in the 1900s, influenced by photography, cinema and other ways of framing a whole scene.',
  56: 'The term comes from timed games and performances divided into two halves. Half-time is the interval at the midpoint, when play pauses before the second half begins.',
  57: '“Double vision” is the everyday name for diplopia, a condition in which one object appears as two. It later became a playful metaphor for any duplicated or confusing view.',
  58: 'The wording is famously found in the King James Bible’s version of Isaiah 52:8: watchers “shall see eye to eye.” Its modern meaning shifted from seeing together to agreeing completely.',
  59: 'Something “up in the air” is suspended and has not yet landed. That physical uncertainty produced the figurative meaning of a plan or decision that remains unsettled.',
  60: 'Cold represents discomfort and a lack of welcome. By the 1800s, leaving someone “out in the cold” meant excluding them from shelter, benefits or the warmth of a group.',
  61: 'The phrase comes from theatres and public venues. When every seat was taken but more customers could enter, signs advertised “standing room only.”',
  62: 'This is a welcoming, literal expression rather than an old metaphor. It says that a space or group already has occupants but can still accommodate one additional person.',
  63: '“Six feet under” refers to burial, but graves have not always followed one universal six-foot rule. The exact wording became a common euphemism for being dead during the 1800s.',
  64: 'People have long described feelings as “mixed” when different emotions occur together. The phrase has no single known inventor; it is a straightforward extension of physically mixing separate ingredients.',
  65: 'Air suggests lightness and freedom from weight. By the 1800s, “walking on air” was being used for feeling so delighted that an ordinary step seemed to float above the ground.',
  66: 'The saying expresses an old philosophical idea: willpower or thought can overcome physical difficulty. The compact wording “mind over matter” became popular in the 1800s.',
  67: 'To be “second to” someone is to rank below them. “Second to none” reverses that comparison and has been used for centuries to mean equal to the very best.',
  68: 'One person among a million is exceptionally rare. The expression grew as “a million” became an everyday symbol for an almost unimaginably large number; no single coinage is firmly established.',
  69: 'The phrase comes from relationships maintained across a large physical distance, especially as modern travel and communication made them more common. It is descriptive rather than an ancient idiom.',
  70: 'Things that move in different directions become farther apart. English uses that simple physical image for relationships or opinions that gradually become less close.',
  71: '“Quarters” can mean living space or a position occupied by people. “Close quarters” described cramped nearness and also close-range fighting before gaining its broader modern use.',
  72: '“Order” has long meant an arranged sequence as well as proper working condition. Something out of order is either in the wrong sequence or not functioning as it should.',
  73: 'A corner can hide something that is physically close but not yet visible. “Just around the corner” therefore became a natural metaphor for an event expected very soon.',
  74: 'Cutting across a corner shortens a physical route. The phrase became a warning that saving time, effort or money this way may also skip an important part of doing the job properly.',
  75: 'The image is deliberately extreme: bending backwards is difficult and unnatural. By the early 1900s it had become a way to describe making an exceptional effort to help or accommodate someone.',
  76: 'A roof represents the home or building it covers. Saying people are “under one roof” simply gathers everyone beneath the same shelter, then extends that image to living or working together.',
  77: 'Important discussions have often been held with doors closed to keep outsiders from hearing. The literal practice produced the modern meaning: privately and away from public observation.',
  78: '“Open secret” is an intentional contradiction: the information is officially secret but widely known. Versions of the paradox have appeared in European languages for centuries, with English use established by the 1800s.',
  79: 'A hat is an easy imaginary hiding place for information. The expression has been used since the 1800s to tell someone to keep knowledge concealed and not pass it on.',
  80: 'The modern expression is American and became established in the second half of the 1900s. Its exact inventor is unknown; the absurd image captures a huge, obvious problem that everyone chooses not to mention.',
  81: 'A fish cannot function normally outside water, making it an old and obvious image for discomfort. English comparisons between an ill-at-ease person and a fish out of water go back to the Middle Ages.',
  82: 'The phrase has been used since at least the 1600s, but its true origin is unknown. Colourful stories about animals falling from roofs or being washed through drains are later guesses, not proven explanations.',
  83: 'Impossible events have long been described by imagining animals doing things they cannot do. English versions involving flying pigs appeared centuries ago; the exact modern wording settled later.',
  84: 'A single drop is almost nothing compared with an ocean. Writers and speakers have used that contrast for centuries to describe an amount too small to affect an enormous need or total.',
  85: 'Clouds are high above ordinary life and can also obscure clear sight. The phrase developed as a playful description of someone absorbed in dreams or thoughts and not paying attention to reality.',
  86: 'The famous wording comes from John Milton’s 1634 masque Comus: a dark cloud may reveal a “silver lining.” The hopeful image later became a familiar proverb about finding something good in a bad situation.',
  87: 'This British expression describes a tiny disturbance made to sound enormous. It belongs to a family of sayings about a “tempest in a teapot” or similarly small container, popular from the 1800s.',
  88: 'Skating on ice that is too thin creates a real danger of breaking through. By the 1800s the image was also being used for any risky situation in which one wrong move could cause trouble.',
  89: 'The exact origin is uncertain. By the 1800s, “cold feet” was being used for a sudden loss of courage, as if fear had physically stopped someone from stepping forward.',
  90: 'Heat and a flushed neck are visible signs of anger or embarrassment. The phrase became common in the 1800s for someone agitated enough to feel hot beneath their collar.',
  91: '“Pressed” has long meant put under pressure or squeezed by a shortage. Being pressed for time therefore means feeling the pressure of too little time before a deadline.',
  92: 'The idea is ancient. The Latin words tempus fugit—“time flees”—come from the Roman poet Virgil, and English later turned the same image into the familiar “time flies.”',
  93: 'This is a modern extension of the idea that time is always moving forward. Trying to finish before a deadline becomes a race against an opponent that cannot stop.',
  94: 'English speakers have called unproductive activity a “waste of time” since at least the 1600s. It treats time as a limited resource that can be spent well or thrown away.',
  95: 'The phrase comes from the circular face and endlessly moving hands of a clock. Working “around the clock” means continuing through the complete day and night without a normal stop.',
  96: 'A distant light has long represented hope after darkness or confinement. The exact source of this wording is uncertain, though tunnel and railway imagery helped make it familiar in the 1800s and 1900s.',
  97: 'Tunnel vision first named a medical loss of peripheral sight, leaving only a narrow central view. During the 1900s it became a metaphor for concentrating so narrowly that other possibilities are missed.',
  98: 'The expression grew with modern systems of signs, radio and electronic communication. When different signals point to different meanings, the receiver cannot tell which message to trust.',
  99: 'Telegraph and telephone systems could produce the wrong message when wires were mistakenly connected. In the early 1900s, “crossed wires” became a common metaphor for misunderstanding one another.',
  100: 'The phrase probably comes from ropes, threads and other work that leaves unsecured ends needing to be tied. It broadened to mean the small unfinished parts of any task or story.',
  101: 'A string can physically connect one thing to another. During the 1800s, “strings” also came to mean hidden influence, control or conditions—so strings attached are obligations that come with an offer.',
  102: 'The image compares connected ideas to railway carriages following one another. “Train” had already meant a connected sequence before railways, and philosophers were writing about a train of thoughts by the 1600s.',
  103: 'Writers have compared ideas to nourishment for centuries. “Food for thought” treats an interesting subject as something the mind can consume, digest and continue working on.',
  104: 'Depth has long represented seriousness and complexity: a deep subject requires looking beneath the surface. “Deep thought” is a natural extension of that old metaphor rather than a phrase with one inventor.',
  105: 'A second thought is a later reconsideration of an earlier choice. English has used “second thoughts” for doubt or a changed mind since at least the 1600s.',
  106: 'The wish for inner calm is ancient, and “peace of mind” appears in English religious and philosophical writing for centuries. It means peace located within one’s thoughts rather than peace between nations.',
  107: 'This humorous expression became common in the 1900s. “Spread” refers to the weight people may gain around the waist during middle age, not to a formal medical condition.',
  108: 'Calling a large amount “a fortune” has been common for centuries. “A small fortune” is deliberately ironic: the amount is smaller than a true fortune but still surprisingly expensive or valuable.',
  109: '“Big cheese” became American slang for an important person in the early 1900s. Its deeper origin is disputed; the popular claim that it comes directly from the Hindi or Urdu word chiz is possible but unproven.',
  110: 'The proverb rests on an old observation that wealth has influence. Versions of “money talks” appeared in English centuries ago, long before it became a common modern catchphrase.',
  111: '“Cash flow” began as a business and accounting term in the 1900s. It pictures money moving into and out of an organisation like a current of water.',
  112: 'Poultry keepers once left a real or artificial egg in a nest to encourage a hen to lay more. By the 1600s, “nest egg” had become a metaphor for savings kept to grow over time.',
  113: 'The phrase comes from gambling houses: a player who won more money than the house could pay could literally break the bank. It later broadened to mean exhausting anyone’s supply of money.',
  114: 'The phrase comes from labour activist Joe Hill’s 1911 song “The Preacher and the Slave.” Its promise of “pie in the sky” mocked rewards offered only in a distant afterlife instead of help in the present.',
  115: '“A piece of cake” became American slang for an easy task in the 1900s. Cake already represented something pleasant and easily enjoyed; the exact person who first made the comparison is unknown.',
  116: 'The comparison was already familiar in English by the 1500s. A tiny needle hidden in a mass of hay became the perfect image for something almost impossible to locate.',
  117: 'The wording comes from the Bible. In Matthew 7:15, false prophets are described as appearing in sheep’s clothing while inwardly being ravenous wolves.',
  118: 'The expression became popular in the 1800s for a shameful family secret kept hidden from outsiders. A closet provided the imagined hiding place, while a skeleton represented something long concealed.',
  119: 'The image comes from lighting both ends of a candle, which produces more light briefly but uses it up twice as fast. The saying is older, but Edna St. Vincent Millay’s 1920 poem made the image especially famous.',
  120: 'English versions of this proverb have circulated since at least the 1500s. Reversing a horse and cart represents doing tasks in the wrong order and expecting the arrangement to work.',
  121: 'The “apple” of the eye was an old name for the pupil, once imagined as a small round object. Biblical translations helped preserve the phrase as a term for someone precious and carefully protected.',
  122: 'This American expression appeared in the 1800s. It is often linked to a challenge in which someone carried a wood chip on the shoulder and dared others to knock it off, though the exact history is not completely settled.',
  123: 'The heart has long represented emotion, while a sleeve leaves something plainly visible. Shakespeare used the full image in Othello, where Iago speaks of wearing his heart upon his sleeve.',
  124: 'The phrase makes hesitation physical: a tied tongue cannot move freely enough to speak. English has described people as tongue-tied for centuries, including in early modern literature.',
  125: 'This American expression became common in the 1900s. It pictures an embarrassing remark as a physical blunder so awkward that the speaker has somehow put a foot into their own mouth.',
  126: 'Soldiers were recorded chewing bullets to avoid crying out during punishment, and later stories linked bullets with enduring battlefield pain. By the late 1800s the phrase meant facing hardship with courage.',
  127: '“Spill the beans” is American slang from the early 1900s. The popular ancient-Greek voting story lacks evidence; the modern sense grew from “spill” already meaning to let information out.',
  128: '“Crack” can mean forcing something closed to open. By the 1800s, writers were using the image for finally producing a smile on an otherwise serious face.',
  129: 'This American expression became common in the early 1900s. Stories connecting it to military dismissal ceremonies are widely repeated but poorly supported, so its exact origin remains uncertain.',
  130: 'The image comes directly from accurate carpentry: a well-aimed hammer strikes the nail squarely on its head. English has used it figuratively for identifying the exact point since at least the 1500s.',
  131: 'The saying is linked to Indigenous North American peace-making traditions in which weapons were put away or buried. English settlers recorded versions of the image in the 1600s, though details vary among nations and accounts.',
  132: 'The phrase comes from controlling a team of horses before they move too soon. It became popular American advice in the 1800s: pause, restrain your excitement and wait.',
  133: '“Kick the bucket” was recorded as slang for dying by the late 1700s. Its exact origin is debated; one strong possibility uses an older meaning of “bucket,” a beam from which slaughtered animals were hung.',
  134: 'The phrase was in print by the 1700s, but its source is not certain. The market-swindle story—revealing a cat substituted for a pig in a bag—is plausible, while the naval-whip explanation fits the meaning less well.',
  135: 'This American frontier expression comes from hunting dogs following an animal’s scent to a tree. If the quarry had escaped elsewhere, the dogs were literally barking at the wrong tree.',
  136: 'Hunters and beaters disturb bushes to drive hidden birds or animals into the open. “Beating around the bush” shifted the action away from the target and became a metaphor for avoiding the main point.',
  137: 'Fire grows when more fuel is added. That simple physical fact has long served as a metaphor for making anger, conflict or another bad situation more intense.',
  138: 'Armies have sometimes destroyed bridges after crossing them to prevent pursuit. Figuratively, burning your bridges removes any route back and makes a decision difficult or impossible to reverse.',
  139: 'A bandwagon originally carried the band in an American circus parade. Politicians adopted the eye-catching wagons during the late 1800s, and climbing aboard became a metaphor for joining a cause once it looked popular.',
  140: 'Pulling a plug can drain a container or disconnect electrical equipment. During the 1900s, the phrase broadened to mean stopping a project, system or life-support process.',
  141: 'This comes from boxing. A trainer could throw a towel or sponge into the ring to signal that their fighter was giving up, protecting them from further punishment.',
  142: 'Black wool was less easily dyed and therefore once considered less valuable than white wool. By the 1700s, a “black sheep” meant the disreputable or troublesome member of a group or family.',
  143: 'The modern figurative use comes from an 1807 political story by William Cobbett, who described a smoked red herring dragging attention onto a false trail. Claims that hunters routinely used herrings this way are doubtful.',
  144: 'European writers connected greenish skin with sickness, jealousy and troubled emotion for centuries. Shakespeare helped make the colour famous by calling jealousy the “green-eyed monster” in Othello.',
  145: 'White traditionally suggested innocence or harmlessness. By the 1700s, a “white lie” meant a minor or well-intentioned untruth, contrasted with a darker and more malicious deception.',
  146: 'The phrase originally referred to being caught with blood still on one’s hands after a crime. Forms of “red hand” appear in Scottish legal and historical writing centuries before the modern wording settled.',
  147: 'A blind spot is literally the point in the eye’s visual field where the optic nerve leaves the retina. The scientific term later became a metaphor for something a person repeatedly fails to notice.',
  148: 'A window opens for only a limited time before closing. The modern business and political phrase turns that brief opening into a chance that must be taken while it remains available.',
  149: 'Keys open locks, so they have symbolised access and solutions for centuries. Calling something “the key to success” means it unlocks the desired result.',
  150: 'Stepping stones are real stones placed so people can cross shallow water or difficult ground. Their useful intermediate role made them a natural metaphor for a stage leading toward a larger goal.',
  151: 'A fork is a place where one road divides into separate routes. It has long served as an image for a decision where choosing one direction means leaving another behind.',
  152: 'Fighting uphill puts an army at a physical disadvantage against opponents above. The military image broadened into a metaphor for any difficult struggle against unfavourable conditions.',
  153: 'Tightrope walkers must move carefully because a small mistake can be disastrous. The circus act became a metaphor for managing a risky situation with little room for error.',
  154: 'The phrase comes from performers who balance objects or themselves. It became a metaphor for keeping competing duties, interests or pressures under control at the same time.',
  155: 'Only a small part of a floating iceberg is visible above water. During the 1900s this became a familiar metaphor for evidence that reveals only a much larger hidden problem.',
  156: 'Sailors and weather-watchers have long noticed that an eerie stillness may arrive before severe weather. The observation became a metaphor for a peaceful pause before trouble or intense activity.',
  157: 'The eye is the relatively calm centre of a strong tropical cyclone, surrounded by the eyewall’s violent winds. Meteorology supplied a vivid phrase for calm located inside turmoil.',
  158: '“On cloud nine” became American slang for extreme happiness in the mid-1900s. Many numbered-cloud explanations have been proposed, but none has enough evidence to settle its exact origin.',
  159: 'A parade represents celebration and public happiness; rain can spoil it. The American expression became common in the 1900s for someone who ruins another person’s pleasure or plans.',
  160: 'A person climbing away from a tree’s trunk onto a limb has less support and greater risk. By the 1800s, going “out on a limb” meant taking a vulnerable position without much backing.',
  161: 'A growing tree sends branches away from its central trunk. The natural image became a metaphor for expanding into new activities, places or subjects.',
  162: 'Roots are hidden foundations from which a plant grows. English has long used them metaphorically for the deepest underlying cause of a problem rather than its visible symptoms.',
  163: 'Genealogies have been drawn as branching trees since the Middle Ages. Ancestors form the trunk or roots while descendants spread into branches, creating the modern family tree.',
  164: '“Leaf” is an old word for a page of a book. Turning over a new leaf therefore meant beginning on a fresh page, and by the 1500s it was being used for changing one’s behaviour.',
  165: 'The Moon is an impossibly high target, so being “over the moon” exaggerates happiness beyond ordinary limits. The image was familiar from nursery-rhyme tradition before its modern emotional use became widespread.',
  166: 'The phrase comes from contracts and advertisements set in very small type. Important restrictions could be physically harder to notice, so “fine print” became shorthand for easily missed conditions.',
  167: 'To be “at cross purposes” means people’s aims point across rather than alongside one another. The English expression has described conflicting intentions and misunderstandings since the 1600s.',
  168: '“Double-cross” became underworld and sporting slang for betrayal in the 1800s. Several elaborate origin stories exist, but the evidence does not identify one certain event or system that created it.',
  169: 'The phrase comes from placing one’s toes exactly against a marked starting or formation line. It means obeying a standard or rule; “tow the line” is a later misspelling.',
  170: '“Duty” has long meant an obligation attached to a role or office. The “line of duty” became official language for actions carried out as part of military, police or other public service.',
  171: 'Cover can hide what lies beneath it. “Undercover” developed in the 1800s for secret work performed behind a concealed identity, especially by police and investigators.',
  172: 'This comes from early printing. Capital letters were traditionally stored in the upper of two type cases, while small letters were kept in the lower case.',
  173: 'Star ratings became a popular way to rank hotels, restaurants and services during the 1900s. Five stars came to represent the highest standard and exceptionally attentive treatment.',
  174: '“Average” became a mathematical term for a central or typical value. Something below average falls beneath that benchmark, so the phrase naturally expanded into everyday comparison.',
  175: 'This is a commercial term for an item requested by a customer but not currently available. The order stays recorded “back” in the seller’s system until stock arrives.',
  176: 'Looking back is a literal physical action that became a metaphor for remembering or reviewing the past. It has no single known inventor.',
  177: 'This is an ordinary sequence instruction: one person speaks first and another repeats afterward. Its familiar wording has no single traceable origin.',
  178: 'Just as below average means lower than a benchmark, above average means higher than the mathematical or expected norm. It is a direct comparison rather than a separately coined idiom.',
  179: 'The phrase refers to the old belief that an ostrich hides its head in sand when frightened. Ostriches do not actually escape danger this way, but the mistaken image became a metaphor for refusing to face facts.',
  180: 'This is an emphatic extension of “nowhere,” meaning in no identifiable place. It developed naturally as a way to say that a missing person or object cannot be located.',
  181: 'Turning back means reversing one’s direction or decision. Saying there is “no turning back” removes that option and marks a choice as irreversible.',
  182: '“Space invader” combines the older science-fiction idea of invaders from space with the title of Taito’s hugely influential 1978 arcade game Space Invaders. It can also jokingly describe someone occupying another person’s space.',
  183: 'Physical objects fall apart when their pieces separate. English extended the image to relationships, plans and emotions that lose their structure or stability.',
  184: 'A person one step ahead is literally slightly farther along a path. The travelling image became a metaphor for having a small advantage in knowledge, preparation or progress.',
  185: 'This is the reverse of being a step ahead. It describes someone following just behind another person’s progress, plans or understanding.',
  186: 'Strings can tether an object or let someone control it like a puppet. “No strings attached” became a common way to promise that an offer carries no hidden control, obligation or condition.',
  187: 'A “point” can be both a small mark and the central meaning of an argument. Missing the point therefore became a compact way to describe failing to understand what matters most.',
  188: 'A blessing is normally wholly good, but real benefits can bring disadvantages with them. “Mixed blessing” became established in the 1800s for something containing both welcome and unwelcome results.',
  189: 'A “close call” is a danger or failure avoided by a very narrow margin. The expression became common in the 1800s, although whether “call” came from judging, signalling or another use is not certain.',
  190: 'Pandora appears in ancient Greek mythology. In Hesiod’s telling she opens a sealed jar—not a box—and releases hardships into the world; later translations and artworks turned the container into the familiar “Pandora’s box.”',
  191: 'The comparison appeared in English during the 1800s. A large bull surrounded by fragile china became an obvious picture of a clumsy person causing damage in a delicate situation.',
  192: 'Ancient and medieval writers repeated a belief that crocodiles wept while killing or eating their prey. The false natural-history story produced “crocodile tears” for an insincere display of sorrow.',
  193: 'A hidden snake has represented treachery since ancient literature. The grass conceals the danger until it strikes, making the phrase a warning about a deceitful person who appears harmless.',
  194: 'A fly can remain unnoticed while seeing and hearing everything in a room. The phrase became common in the 1900s as a wish to observe a private event without taking part or being detected.',
  195: 'Birds see the ground from high above, so a bird’s-eye view means an elevated overview. Mapmakers and artists used the term before aircraft made the perspective an everyday reality.',
  196: 'This teasing question was in use by the 1800s, but its exact origin is unknown. Stories involving punishment with cat-o’-nine-tails whips or cats stealing tongues have no convincing evidence.',
  197: 'Nervousness can create real fluttering sensations in the abdomen. In the early 1900s, English speakers began comparing that feeling to butterflies moving inside the stomach.',
  198: 'Ants are small, restless and constantly moving. By the 1800s, imagining them inside someone’s clothing had become a humorous description of fidgeting and being unable to sit still.',
  199: 'The proverb was recorded in English in the 1600s, including in John Ray’s collection of proverbs. A bird that begins hunting early has the best chance of finding the worm first.',
  200: 'Owls are active at night, so English speakers naturally used “night owl” for a person who stays awake or works late. The comparison has been used for several centuries.',
  201: 'Bees appear constantly occupied as they fly between flowers and a hive. English has used their activity as a model of human busyness for centuries; no single person is known to have coined the comparison.',
  202: 'A duck is naturally at ease swimming almost as soon as it enters water. By the 1800s, English speakers were using that effortless adjustment as a comparison for taking naturally to a new activity.',
  203: 'Seizing a bull by its horns is an image of confronting the most dangerous part of a problem directly. Versions of the expression circulated in Europe before becoming established in English during the 1700s.',
  204: 'Feathers were historically worn as decorations and sometimes as marks of honour or achievement. Adding one to a cap therefore became a natural metaphor for gaining another distinction.',
  205: 'Producing a rabbit from an apparently empty hat became a famous stage-magic effect in the 1800s. The trick supplied a metaphor for creating a surprising solution from seemingly nowhere.',
  206: 'Shakespeare used “wild-goose chase” in Romeo and Juliet, referring to a kind of erratic horse race rather than hunting birds. It later came to mean a hopeless or pointless pursuit.',
  207: 'A sitting duck is stationary and therefore an easy target for a hunter. The American expression became common in the 1900s for any person or thing left exposed and defenceless.',
  208: 'A monkey clinging to someone’s back became slang for a persistent burden, especially addiction, in the 1800s and 1900s. Its precise first source is uncertain.',
  209: 'The expression comes from ancient fables in which a lion claims most or all of a shared prize because it is the strongest animal. “The lion’s share” therefore means the largest portion.',
  210: 'Colour has long been used to distinguish one horse from another. Older forms such as “a horse of another colour” gradually settled into a phrase meaning an entirely different matter.',
  211: 'The wording is a compact rule about priorities: deal with the most important or earliest matter before everything else. It developed as proverbial advice rather than from one known event.',
  212: 'This expression reassures the final item or person in a list that being mentioned last does not make them less important. It is an old piece of rhetorical wordplay rather than a literal ranking system.',
  213: '“Zero tolerance” became prominent in American policy language during the late 1900s, especially for rules allowing no exceptions. It then spread into schools, workplaces and everyday speech.',
  214: 'The saying grew from older proverbs about a final small load breaking an overburdened animal’s back. The familiar camel-and-straw wording became widespread in the 1800s.',
  215: 'Countdowns became strongly associated with rocket launches through broadcasting and the space age. Calling one “final” heightens the last sequence before an important ending; the 1986 Europe song later made the wording especially famous.',
  216: 'A clock turns a deadline into a visible opponent that cannot be stopped. Racing or working “against the clock” became common as timed sport, work and transport expanded in the 1800s and 1900s.',
  217: 'A “nick” can mean a precisely cut notch or exact point. Arriving “in the nick of time” has meant arriving at the critical moment since at least the 1600s.',
  218: 'A second is already a short unit of time, so splitting it creates an even briefer instant. The expression became common alongside increasingly precise mechanical timekeeping.',
  219: 'American slang connected “the third degree” with severe questioning in the late 1800s. It is often linked to the third degree of Freemasonry, but the exact path into interrogation slang is not certain.',
  220: '“The four corners of the earth” appears in ancient religious writing, including the Bible. The corners represent the world’s furthest directions or extremities, not a modern scientific claim that Earth is square.',
  221: 'A signed cheque with its amount left blank lets another person fill in the sum. That literal financial authority became a metaphor for giving someone unrestricted permission or resources.',
  222: 'Paperwork leaves documents that can be followed from one action or transaction to the next. “Paper trail” became especially common in the 1900s as governments and businesses kept larger formal records.',
  223: 'Legal and government papers were once tied into bundles with red cloth tape. By the 1700s, the visible binding had become a metaphor for obstructive official procedure and bureaucracy.',
  224: 'Modern traffic signals use green to authorise movement. During the 1900s, receiving “the green light” naturally broadened into receiving permission to begin any project or action.',
  225: 'Grey lies between black and white, colours often used for clear opposites. A “grey area” therefore became a place where rules, facts or moral choices are uncertain rather than definite.',
  226: 'The idea came into English from Spanish “sangre azul,” or blue blood, for aristocratic ancestry. Pale skin could make blue veins especially visible, helping associate the colour with privileged families.',
  227: 'The image goes back to the Roman poet Horace, who criticised a showy purple patch sewn onto an otherwise plain work. English later used “purple patch” for an unusually brilliant passage or successful period.',
  228: 'An open book exposes every page to view. By the 1800s, the object had become a metaphor for a person whose thoughts, character or history are easy to read.',
  229: 'A closed book hides its pages and cannot be read. The phrase became the natural opposite of “an open book,” describing a subject or person that remains mysterious.',
  230: 'Small insects and larvae really do damage old books, although “bookworm” also described an intensely devoted reader by the 1500s. The humorous double meaning has survived ever since.',
  231: 'Publishers and reviewers began calling gripping books “page-turners” during the 1900s. The name pictures a reader so absorbed that they keep turning pages rapidly.',
  232: 'Chapters and verses provide exact locations in the Bible and other structured texts. Giving something “chapter and verse” therefore came to mean supplying complete, precise supporting detail.',
  233: 'To “read” can mean interpreting people as well as printed words. The modern advice to “read the room” asks someone to notice a group’s mood before deciding how to behave.',
  234: 'Old lotteries included tickets that carried no prize or information—a blank could literally be drawn. The phrase broadened into being unable to find an answer or remember something.',
  235: 'Forms and exercises often leave empty spaces for missing information. “Fill in the blanks” moved naturally from that literal instruction to supplying missing parts of a story or explanation.',
  236: 'A bridge physically connects two sides separated by a gap. The engineering image became an everyday metaphor for overcoming a difference in knowledge, distance or opinion.',
  237: 'The familiar wording echoes Robert Frost’s 1915 poem “The Road Not Taken,” which describes taking the road “less traveled by.” Popular culture later simplified and reinterpreted the poem’s more ambiguous choice.',
  238: 'A road eventually stops at its destination or boundary. “The end of the road” therefore became a metaphor for reaching the point where progress, options or a relationship can go no farther.',
  239: 'Travel on an uneven road is uncomfortable and unpredictable. The literal experience became an obvious metaphor for a difficult period filled with setbacks.',
  240: 'The physical middle of a road lies between its two edges. During the 1900s, “middle of the road” also became political and cultural language for a moderate, non-extreme position.',
  241: 'A beaten track is a path worn clear by many travellers or animals. Going off it means leaving the familiar route, which produced the figurative sense of doing something unusual or less explored.',
  242: 'To haul is to pull or transport a load, often over distance. “The long haul” developed in transport and labour before becoming a metaphor for sustained effort over a lengthy period.',
  243: 'A shortcut is literally a route shorter than the usual one. The word dates back centuries and later expanded to any quicker method of reaching a result.',
  244: 'A dead-end road offers no route onward. By the early 1900s, the traffic term was also describing jobs, plans and situations with no possibility of progress.',
  245: 'The older proverb warned, “Speak of the devil and he will appear.” It reflected the superstitious idea that naming an evil presence might summon it; modern use is usually humorous when someone arrives just after being mentioned.',
  246: 'The expression comes from the biblical parable in Matthew 20, where labourers hired at the eleventh hour still receive wages. It came to mean the latest possible moment before an opportunity ends.',
  247: '“The times” can mean current fashions, ideas and developments. Someone behind them is figuratively lagging behind the present age.',
  248: '“Two-time” became American slang for deceiving someone, especially by maintaining two romantic relationships at once. It was established in popular speech by the early 1900s.',
  249: 'Placing DAY first inside and then outside creates a repeated cycle. The expression has long meant continuously, every day, with little variation.',
  250: 'This is the weekly counterpart of “day in, day out.” It emphasises something continuing through one week after another without a break.',
  251: 'A month made entirely of Sundays would feel impossibly long because Sundays arrive only once each week. The exaggerated expression has described a very long time since at least the 1800s.',
  252: '“The year dot” is chiefly British and Australian informal language for the earliest imaginable time. The dot acts like the zero point at the beginning of a timeline.',
  253: 'Storytellers have opened tales with versions of “once upon a time” since the Middle Ages. It deliberately places the story in an unspecified and often magical past.',
  254: 'A clock has hands, creating an easy visual pun with human hands. The phrase can also suggest spare time available for someone to use.',
  255: 'Timed contests encouraged people to imagine finishing before a clock could defeat them. “Beat the clock” became especially common through racing, games and deadline-driven work in the 1900s.',
  256: 'A dark cloud visible at the horizon can warn that bad weather is approaching. The observation became a metaphor for a future problem that is beginning to appear.',
  257: 'The phrase comes from the biblical Book of Daniel. Mysterious writing appears on King Belshazzar’s wall and foretells the fall of his kingdom, so the image now means a clear warning of approaching trouble.',
  258: 'Being “born with a silver spoon in one’s mouth” was recorded by the 1700s. Silver tableware represented a wealthy household, so the spoon became shorthand for privilege inherited from birth.',
  259: '“Golden handshake” emerged in 20th-century business language for a generous payment made when an executive leaves a company. Gold suggests exceptional financial value, while the handshake represents the agreement.',
  260: 'An iron fist combines the human symbol of control with a metal associated with strength. Ruling with one means exercising authority harshly and without flexibility.',
  261: 'Steel is strong, resilient and difficult to bend. Comparing nerves to steel became a compact way to praise someone who remains calm and courageous under pressure.',
  262: 'Management consultant Marilyn Loden used “glass ceiling” at a 1978 conference to describe invisible barriers blocking women’s advancement. The phrase quickly spread to other forms of workplace inequality.',
  263: 'Destroying a bridge after crossing removes the route back. Armies have used the tactic literally, while “burning bridges” became a metaphor for damaging relationships or making decisions irreversible.',
  264: '“Memory lane” imagines recollection as a path back through the past. A popular 1920s song titled “Memory Lane” helped establish the phrase in modern English.',
  265: 'The insult points out the absurdity of one blackened cooking pot criticising an equally black kettle. English versions have circulated since the 1600s, including in early translations of Don Quixote.',
  266: 'The proverb contrasts a bird already safely held with two that might escape from a bush. Forms of the advice are ancient, and an English version was recorded in the Middle Ages.',
  267: 'Breaking ice literally opens a frozen surface so movement can begin. During the 1600s and later, the action became a metaphor for overcoming initial social stiffness or silence.',
  268: 'Eggshells crack under very little pressure, forcing anyone crossing them to step with extreme care. The image has described tense situations requiring caution since the 1800s.',
  269: 'The proverb warns that one accident can destroy everything when all eggs share a single basket. English versions were circulating by the 1600s and were reinforced by similar European sayings.',
  270: '“Couch potato” is American slang from the 1970s for someone who spends long periods sitting and watching television. The comic image combines inactivity on a sofa with a motionless potato.',
  271: 'A cherry is a traditional finishing decoration on desserts and drinks. Putting one on top became a metaphor for a final attractive detail that makes something already good even better.',
  272: '“Bananas” became American slang for crazy or wildly excited during the 1900s. Its exact origin is uncertain, though comic performance and popular entertainment helped spread it.',
  273: 'A hot potato is painful to hold, so people pass it quickly from hand to hand. The children’s game and literal image produced a metaphor for a controversial problem nobody wants to keep.',
  274: 'Tinned sardines are laid extremely close together to use every bit of space. People were being compared with tightly packed sardines by the early 1800s.',
  275: 'Older English used “other fish to fry” for more important business; a form appears in an early-1700s translation of Don Quixote. “Bigger fish” later became the more familiar variation.',
  276: 'A bee trapped inside a bonnet would be impossible to ignore. By the 1700s, having “a bee in one’s bonnet” meant being preoccupied with a persistent idea.',
  277: 'Horse-racing bettors valued information from trainers and stable workers closest to the animal. News “straight from the horse’s mouth” came to mean information received from the most direct and reliable source.',
  278: '“All over” has long meant scattered across an entire space. Adding “the place” strengthens the image of things being widespread, disorganised or difficult to locate.',
  279: 'The phrase plays with physical length: a long account is reduced to its short and important conclusion. Versions have been used since at least the 1800s.',
  280: 'Writers have long noticed that similar events and mistakes recur across generations. “History repeats itself” condenses that observation; it is related to, but not identical with, several famous philosophical quotations.',
  281: 'Business can mean any matter requiring attention, not only commerce. Something left unfinished therefore remains an obligation that must eventually be completed.',
  282: 'The expression comes directly from elementary arithmetic. Combining two and two gives an obvious result, so the calculation became a metaphor for drawing a sensible conclusion from available clues.',
  283: 'This is a literal description of sequence: one event follows the previous one. Its repetition makes it useful for describing a continuous stream of occurrences.',
  284: 'The phrase shortens the old proverb “he who laughs last laughs best.” The final laugh belongs to the person whose eventual success overturns earlier mockery.',
  285: 'A play on words exploits two meanings, similar sounds or an unexpected arrangement of language. The expression has described puns and verbal wit for centuries.',
  286: 'Translators and witnesses may reproduce something “word for word,” preserving each word in the same order. It has long meant an exact verbal copy rather than a summary.',
  287: 'Words can seem to disappear when surprise or emotion leaves someone unable to speak. The expression turns that temporary inability into the image of language being lost.',
  288: '“Mind the gap” became internationally famous as a London Underground safety warning introduced in 1969. It tells passengers to notice the space between a train and platform.',
  289: 'The saying places necessary work before enjoyment. It belongs to a long tradition of moral advice about completing duties before taking leisure.',
  290: 'People travelling in the same boat share the same risks if it succeeds or sinks. English has used the boat as a metaphor for a shared situation since at least the 1600s.',
  291: 'Sudden movement can destabilise a small boat and endanger everyone aboard. “Rock the boat” therefore became advice not to disturb a stable situation or cause unnecessary trouble.',
  292: 'Missing a departing boat once meant losing transport or an opportunity to travel. The literal disappointment became a general metaphor for acting too late.',
  293: 'A moving object or person can disturb otherwise calm water and create waves. The image became a metaphor for causing noticeable change, reaction or controversy.',
  294: 'A person unexpectedly thrown into deep water must either swim or sink. The stark alternatives became a metaphor for being forced to succeed without gradual preparation.',
  295: 'Someone entering unfamiliar water may first test its depth, current or temperature. The cautious action became a metaphor for trying a situation before making a full commitment.',
  296: 'Moving with a current is easier than fighting against it. “Go with the flow” became a modern metaphor for accepting events and adapting without unnecessary resistance.',
  297: 'Keeping one’s head above water is the minimum needed to avoid drowning. The physical struggle became a metaphor for barely managing work, debt or another difficult situation.',
  298: 'Hot water can burn and is unpleasant to be trapped in. English has used being “in hot water” for being in serious trouble since at least the 1500s.',
  299: 'Ships, buildings and people that survive a storm endure violent conditions without being destroyed. The experience supplied a metaphor for surviving a difficult period until it passes.',
  300: 'The sarcastic command imagines crying so excessively that the tears form a river. Arthur Hamilton’s 1953 song “Cry Me a River,” made famous by Julie London, helped popularise the wording.',
  301: 'Benjamin Disraeli used “dark horse” in his 1831 novel The Young Duke for an unexpected winner in a horse race. It soon broadened to any little-known competitor who surprises everyone.',
  302: 'A bull charging a gate suggests reckless force with no pause for caution. The comparison became especially familiar in British and Australian English, although no single event is known to have created it.',
  303: 'Horned animals such as deer and cattle literally lock horns while fighting for dominance. English has used that physical struggle as a metaphor for serious conflict since the 1800s.',
  304: 'Lions have been called kings of beasts since ancient fables and heraldry. “King of the jungle” is a later popular title—even though wild lions mainly live in grasslands rather than jungles.',
  305: 'People once believed a bee returning to its hive flew in a perfectly direct line. American English was using “bee-line” for a straight, rapid route by the early 1800s.',
  306: '“No flies on him” was 1800s slang for someone too alert to be fooled or taken advantage of. It praised quickness and awareness rather than literal cleanliness.',
  307: 'A butterfly flits lightly from flower to flower. By the late 1800s and early 1900s, a “social butterfly” similarly described someone who moves easily among many people and gatherings.',
  308: 'The short end of a divided stick gives its holder the poorer share. The expression developed from older “wrong end of the stick” language, but its exact literal starting point is uncertain.',
  309: 'Ancient writers repeated a story that a tiny copy of Homer’s Iliad could fit inside a nutshell. Later literary uses, including Shakespeare’s Hamlet, helped the nutshell become an image for something compressed into very little space.',
  310: 'Playwright John Dennis devised a new thunder sound effect for his 1709 play Appius and Virginia. When another theatre production reused it, he complained that they had “stolen my thunder,” creating the famous phrase.',
  311: 'The proverb treats lightning as an event too extraordinary to recur in exactly the same place. It was common by the 1800s, although modern observation shows that lightning often does strike the same tall object repeatedly.',
  312: 'A clear blue sky gives no warning of a lightning bolt. Writers in the 1800s turned that shocking contrast into “a bolt from the blue” for a completely unexpected event.',
  313: 'Thunderstorms are dark, violent and threatening. Comparing an angry expression with thunder became a vivid way to describe a face that visibly warns of bad temper.',
  314: 'Heavy snow can bury and overwhelm whatever lies beneath it. “Snowed under” became a figurative description of being overloaded with work during the late 1800s.',
  315: 'An outdoor event promised “rain or shine” will happen regardless of the weather. The practical wording became a broader promise to continue despite changing circumstances.',
  316: 'In golf, a hole in one occurs when the first stroke sends the ball directly into the cup. The rare achievement has been recorded since the early development of modern golf.',
  317: 'A dishonest card player could hide a valuable ace inside a sleeve for use at the right moment. The cheating image became a metaphor for a secret advantage or plan held in reserve.',
  318: 'Players who place their cards face-up on a table reveal what they hold. By the 1800s, “cards on the table” was also describing open intentions and honest disclosure.',
  319: 'Playing cards can be balanced into an impressive structure, but one small disturbance may collapse it. A “house of cards” therefore became a metaphor for an elaborate but dangerously fragile system.',
  320: 'A deal breaker is a condition serious enough to stop an agreement entirely. The term grew from American business and negotiation language during the later 1900s.',
  321: 'The phrase appeared in the work of English clergyman James Hervey in 1746. It describes a seeming misfortune that conceals an unexpected benefit.',
  322: 'Gold has served as a standard of exceptional value for thousands of years. Saying someone is worth their weight in gold exaggerates that person’s usefulness by valuing their whole body as precious metal.',
  323: 'The heart has long represented character and generosity, while gold represents exceptional worth. Shakespeare used “a heart of gold” in Henry V, helping preserve the compliment.',
  324: 'Gold symbolises rarity, wealth and high value. A “golden opportunity” is therefore a chance imagined as unusually precious and too valuable to waste.',
  325: 'Miners “strike” a mineral deposit when digging suddenly reaches it. Gold-rush language turned “strike gold” into both a literal discovery and a metaphor for unexpected success.',
  326: 'Aeschylus described a path of costly purple-red fabric in the ancient play Agamemnon. The modern ceremonial red carpet developed much later and became a standard way to honour important arrivals.',
  327: '“Paint the town red” became American slang for a wild celebration during the 1880s. A famous story about drunken aristocrats painting buildings in Melton Mowbray is colourful but not proven as the phrase’s source.',
  328: 'Important feast days were printed in red ink on medieval and early-modern calendars. A “red-letter day” therefore came to mean a specially important or joyful date.',
  329: 'Versions of being caught between the devil and the deep sea appear in English from the 1600s. The image means choosing between two dangers; later nautical explanations are possible but not securely proven.',
  330: 'Ancient Greek and Latin proverbs already compared escaping one danger only to fall into another. English versions involving a frying pan and fire were established by the 1500s.',
  331: 'Fishermen may keep bait worms tangled inside a container; opening it creates a difficult mess. The figurative “can of worms” became common in American English during the mid-1900s.',
  332: 'Taking a mouthful too large to chew is an obvious warning against attempting more than one can manage. English versions of the proverb have circulated since at least the 1500s.',
  333: 'A painful bite teaches caution when the same danger appears again. Variations of “once bitten, twice shy” existed across Europe before the familiar English wording settled.',
  334: '“Fat chance” is deliberately ironic: “fat” normally suggests abundance, but the phrase means very little chance at all. The sarcastic expression became common around the turn of the 20th century.',
  335: '“Slim chance” uses slim in its ordinary sense of very small or narrow. It became the non-ironic counterpart to the sarcastic “fat chance.”',
  336: 'Odds are the calculated chances for or against an outcome, especially in betting. Acting “against all odds” means succeeding despite every probability appearing unfavourable.',
  337: 'An “odd end” was a leftover piece that did not match the rest. The plural “odds and ends” became established in the 1700s for a miscellaneous collection of small remnants.',
  338: '“At sixes and sevens” has described confusion or disorder since the 1500s. Several stories try to explain the particular numbers, but none has enough evidence to settle the phrase’s exact origin.',
  339: '“To the nines” meant to the highest degree before it was specifically connected with clothing. “Dressed to the nines” appeared in the 1800s; stories about nine yards of cloth or a particular regiment are unsupported.',
  340: 'Ten is the highest score in many decimal rating systems. A “perfect ten” became a familiar description of flawless performance or appearance, especially through judged sport and entertainment.',
  341: 'A coin carries a different design on each face while remaining one object. The image became a metaphor for two contrasting aspects that belong to the same underlying situation.',
  342: 'John Heywood included a version of “a penny for your thought” in his 1546 collection of English proverbs. The small offered coin playfully asks someone to reveal what occupies their mind.',
  343: 'An arm and a leg are body parts no sensible buyer would surrender. The American expression for an outrageously high price became common in the mid-1900s; literal wartime-amputation origin stories lack evidence.',
  344: 'To pull someone’s leg means teasing or deceiving them playfully. It was established by the 1800s, but stories about tripping thieves or dragging execution victims are not supported well enough to prove its origin.',
  345: 'Standing without support is a basic image of independence. The phrase moved naturally from physical balance on one’s feet to financial, emotional or practical self-reliance.',
  346: 'People positioned shoulder to shoulder form a close, solid line. The literal arrangement has long represented unity, cooperation and equal effort.',
  347: 'A hand performs work and can physically help another person. Offering or lending one therefore became an old and direct metaphor for giving assistance.',
  348: 'The phrase comes from horse racing. A jockey certain of victory could lower their hands and relax the reins before the finish, winning “hands down” without maximum effort.',
  349: 'Tied hands cannot act freely. The physical restraint became a metaphor for rules, circumstances or authority preventing someone from doing what they otherwise would.',
  350: 'The idea is ancient: after someone has already been harmed, an additional insult makes the wrong even worse. English forms of “add insult to injury” became common in the 1700s.',
  351: 'Salt in an open wound causes intense pain. “Rub salt in the wound” turned that physical cruelty into a metaphor for making an existing hurt or embarrassment worse.',
  352: 'Doctors have checked the pulse for centuries to judge a patient’s condition. Keeping a finger on it became a metaphor for closely monitoring the health or mood of a changing situation.',
  353: 'Crossing fingers became associated with wishing for luck and protection. Its precise development is uncertain, and popular claims tying it to one secret early-Christian practice are not firmly documented.',
  354: 'A thumb provides a convenient rough measure, so a “rule of thumb” means a practical estimate rather than an exact law. The often-repeated claim that it came from a legal right to beat one’s wife is false.',
  355: 'The image comes from an ancient Greek story in which every stone should be moved while searching for a hidden general. English preserved it as advice to try every possible method.',
  356: 'The “envelope” was originally an aircraft’s charted performance limits. Test pilots pushed that flight envelope, and Tom Wolfe’s 1979 book The Right Stuff helped bring the technical phrase into everyday speech.',
  357: 'A 1941 New Yorker cartoon by Peter Arno showed engineers walking away from a crashed aircraft while one says, “Well, back to the old drawing board.” The joke created the modern phrase for starting a design again.',
  358: 'A chart has a fixed scale and boundary. A result “off the charts” exceeds what the graph was designed to show, producing the modern meaning of something extraordinarily high or extreme.',
  359: 'The phrase echoes Jesus’s teaching in Matthew 5:41: if compelled to go one mile, go two. The extra mile became a metaphor for voluntarily doing more than duty requires.',
  360: 'American baseball clubs began issuing “rain checks” in the 1800s so spectators could return after a game was cancelled by rain. The ticket became a general promise to accept an invitation or service later.',
  361: 'Mud is opaque, so calling something “clear as mud” says the opposite of what the words appear to promise. The humorous contradiction became established in the 1800s.',
  362: 'Ships and armies used coloured flags to display allegiance. Showing one’s “true colours” meant revealing the flag—and therefore the identity or intentions—that had previously been concealed.',
  363: 'Covering cracks with paper or decoration hides visible damage without repairing the structure. The building image became a metaphor for disguising a problem with a superficial solution.',
  364: 'Runners are meant to begin only after the starter’s gun. Someone who moves before it fires has literally jumped the gun, which became a general warning against acting too soon.',
  365: 'Firing a weapon in darkness gives little chance of hitting the intended target. A “shot in the dark” therefore became a guess or attempt made with very little information.',
  366: 'Proverbs have long observed that another field appears greener than one’s own. The modern wording expresses the tendency to imagine other people’s circumstances are better, often without seeing their disadvantages.',
  367: 'A person sitting on a boundary fence has not stepped down onto either side. The physical indecision became an obvious metaphor for refusing to choose between competing positions.',
  368: 'Fences divide neighbours, so repairing one can restore both the boundary and the relationship across it. “Mend fences” became political language in 19th-century America before spreading more widely.',
  369: 'Goalposts define the target and rules of a sport. Moving them during play would make success unfairly harder, so the action became a metaphor for changing requirements after work has begun.',
  370: 'In racket sports, responsibility shifts to the player whose side of the court holds the ball. “The ball is in your court” therefore means the next decision or action belongs to you.',
  371: 'Players who fail to catch or control a ball make a visible mistake. American sport turned “drop the ball” into a general metaphor for neglecting a duty or opportunity.',
  372: 'Ball players must watch the ball closely to react well. Coaches’ literal instruction became everyday advice to remain focused on the most important objective.',
  373: '“Ball game” is American language for the whole situation or contest. Adding “a whole new” means that conditions have changed so much that the old expectations no longer apply.',
  374: 'Baseball fielders must guard or “cover” each base to prevent runners advancing safely. The sporting instruction broadened into making sure every possibility or responsibility is handled.',
  375: 'In baseball, a runner physically reaches and touches a base. American English turned that brief contact into a metaphor for making contact with someone to exchange information.',
  376: 'The home stretch is the final straight section of a racecourse leading toward the finish. It became a metaphor for the last part of any long task or journey.',
  377: 'Something that “hits home” reaches its intended destination with full force. By the 1800s, the phrase was describing remarks or truths that affect someone personally and deeply.',
  378: 'A welcoming temporary place can provide the comfort and familiarity of one’s real home. The phrase became common in travel, hospitality and family life during the 1800s.',
  379: 'Bacon represented valuable food and household livelihood, so bringing it home meant securing the family’s needs. Several sporting and fairground stories are associated with the phrase, but no single one fully explains its development.',
  380: 'A foot wedged into a doorway prevents it closing and creates a chance to enter. Sales work helped make the image a metaphor for gaining an initial opportunity that may lead to something larger.',
  381: 'Directing someone to the exit is a clear act of dismissal. “Show someone the door” became a polite-sounding but firm way to say they were made to leave.',
  382: 'The proverb treats a closed door as a lost opportunity and an opening door as a new one. It is often attributed to Alexander Graham Bell, but similar ideas and wording circulated independently.',
  383: 'An open door permits entry where a closed one blocks it. The physical access became a metaphor for opportunities, acceptance and new possibilities.',
  384: 'Revolving doors, developed for public buildings in the late 1800s, let people enter and leave continuously. The object later became a metaphor for repeated turnover between positions or institutions.',
  385: 'Wind carries away anything light enough to be thrown into it. Older expressions about casting care to the winds developed into “throw caution to the wind” for abandoning restraint and accepting risk.',
  386: 'A favourable wind fills a sailing vessel’s canvas and drives it forward. Having “wind in your sails” therefore means receiving energy, confidence or support that helps progress.',
  387: 'Without wind, a sailing vessel loses its driving force. Taking the wind out of someone’s sails became a metaphor for removing their confidence, momentum or advantage.',
  388: 'The heart has represented belief, intention and emotion for centuries. A “change of heart” therefore means an internal change of feeling or decision rather than a physical replacement.',
  389: 'Fear and suspense can produce pounding sensations that seem to rise into the throat. The bodily experience created the image of having one’s heart in one’s mouth.',
  390: 'The heart normally beats in a steady rhythm, but excitement can make it seem to pause or flutter. “Skip a beat” became a familiar way to describe sudden fear, surprise or attraction.',
  391: 'The bottom of a vessel is its deepest part. Since the heart symbolises sincere feeling, speaking from its bottom means speaking from the deepest and most genuine emotion.',
  392: 'The heart has long represented emotion, while weight represents burden. A “heavy heart” therefore describes grief or reluctance as though sorrow physically weighs the heart down.',
  393: 'Versions of “blood is thicker than water” have compared family bonds with other relationships for centuries. The popular claim that a longer “covenant” quotation was the original is a modern invention, not historical evidence.',
  394: 'Blood, sweat and tears list the bodily costs of extreme effort and suffering. Forms of the trio appeared before Winston Churchill, though his 1940 promise of “blood, toil, tears and sweat” made the idea especially famous.',
  395: 'Someone arguing or straining without success may become breathless enough for their face to redden or appear bluish. The exaggeration became a phrase for continuing pointlessly for a very long time.',
  396: 'Tickling produces laughter and a flushed, rosy colour. By the 1600s, “tickled” could mean delighted, and “tickled pink” later intensified that happiness with the colour of pleasure.',
  397: 'Black ink on white paper creates the clearest traditional written record. “In black and white” therefore came to mean stated plainly, definitely and usually in writing.',
  398: 'A dark mark on a clean record stands out as a visible blemish. The physical image became a metaphor for a fault or event that damages someone’s reputation.',
  399: 'Fear, illness or shock can drain colour from a face until it resembles plain white cloth. Comparing extreme pallor with a sheet became common during the 1800s.',
  400: 'White flags have signalled truce, negotiation or surrender in different cultures for many centuries. International laws of war later formally recognised the flag as protection for someone seeking to communicate.',
  401: 'Anger can feel as though it rises until it bursts through the top of a building. “Hit the roof” became a vivid 20th-century expression for suddenly becoming extremely angry.',
  402: 'Breaking untouched ground is the first physical step in excavation, farming or construction. The action became a metaphor for doing pioneering work in a previously unexplored area.',
  403: 'A fighter or soldier who refuses to retreat continues occupying the same ground. The military image broadened into holding firmly to one’s principles or position.',
  404: 'People standing on the same ground share one physical space. “Common ground” therefore became a metaphor for beliefs or interests on which otherwise different sides can agree.',
  405: 'Trackers have listened against the earth for vibrations from approaching movement. The image developed into keeping closely informed about events, opinion or change.',
  406: 'A small added weight can make a balance scale lean decisively to one side. “Tip the scales” became a metaphor for the factor that changes a close decision or result.',
  407: 'Something suspended by only one thin thread can fall if that final support breaks. The image has described extreme danger or uncertainty since at least the 1500s.',
  408: 'A plot is the connected sequence that makes a story understandable. “Lose the plot” became chiefly British slang in the late 1900s for losing track of events, reason or self-control.',
  409: 'A complicated story gains more material and becomes figuratively thicker as new developments appear. The theatrical and literary phrase was established by the 1800s.',
  410: 'A performer who attracts more attention than the intended star can take the audience’s admiration for themselves. Theatre supplied “steal the show” for unexpectedly becoming the most impressive part.',
  411: 'Circus and theatre managers could not easily stop a performance when trouble occurred, because an audience was waiting and money was at stake. “The show must go on” became a professional rule and then general encouragement to continue.',
  412: 'Stage scenery conceals performers, ropes and workers from an audience. Activity “behind the scenes” therefore became a metaphor for private work that produces a public result.',
  413: 'The centre of a stage is usually the most visible position for a performer. Taking centre stage came to mean receiving the greatest attention in any situation.',
  414: 'Performing before an audience can cause real fear and physical symptoms. “Stage fright” became the standard name for that anxiety during the 1800s.',
  415: 'After a performance, applause may call actors back in front of the curtain to bow. That return is the literal theatre “curtain call.”',
  416: 'A person trapped between an immovable rock and an equally unyielding hard place has no comfortable escape. The American expression became common in the early 1900s.',
  417: 'Pulling wool across someone’s eyes prevents them seeing clearly. The phrase was in use by the 1800s for deliberately deceiving or confusing another person.',
  418: 'Water beads and rolls away from a duck’s naturally water-resistant feathers. The observation became a comparison for criticism or trouble that has no lasting effect.',
  419: 'A square peg cannot fit neatly into a round opening. Writers in the early 1800s used the mismatch for people placed in roles unsuited to their character or abilities.',
  420: 'In Greek mythology, Atlas is condemned to support the heavens, later commonly pictured as carrying Earth. That immense burden inspired the image of the world’s weight resting on someone’s shoulders.',
  421: 'Someone backed against a wall has no room to retreat. The fighting image became a metaphor for being forced into a difficult position with very few choices.',
  422: 'A solid brick wall abruptly stops forward movement. “Hit a brick wall” came to describe encountering an obstacle that effort alone cannot immediately overcome.',
  423: '“Off the wall” emerged from American Black slang and appears in print during the 1950s. Its underlying wall metaphor is uncertain, but the phrase developed meanings such as irrelevant, unconventional or wildly strange.',
  424: 'Persistent irritation can make someone feel desperate enough to climb a wall to escape it. The exaggerated image became a common description of extreme annoyance.',
  425: 'Warnings that walls might hear private speech have circulated in Europe for centuries. Stories crediting a particular palace or ruler are difficult to prove; the phrase simply cautions that secrets may have unseen listeners.',
  426: 'Admiral Horatio Nelson famously raised a telescope to his blind eye during the 1801 Battle of Copenhagen and said he could not see a signal to withdraw. The story helped popularise an older idea of deliberately ignoring what is visible.',
  427: 'Something may contain hidden complexity beyond its first appearance. “More than meets the eye” was in use before Transformers adopted “more than meets the eye” as a famous 1980s slogan.',
  428: 'Hunger and desire can persuade someone to take more food than their stomach can hold. The phrase became a warning that appetite or ambition may exceed actual capacity.',
  429: 'A welcome person or object brings relief to tired or uncomfortable eyes. Versions of “a sight for sore eyes” were established by the 1700s.',
  430: 'Stars have long represented fame, wonder and dazzling excitement. Seeing them in someone’s eyes became an image of ambition, admiration or overwhelming emotion.',
  431: 'English writers were contrasting deeds with speech by the 1600s; Thomas Manton wrote in 1693 that actions “speak much louder than Words.” The modern proverb says behaviour is stronger evidence than promises.',
  432: 'To place words in another person’s mouth is to make them appear to say something they did not choose. The image has appeared in religious, legal and literary writing for centuries.',
  433: 'News and opinion travel informally through people gathered in public streets. “Word on the street” became especially common in 20th-century journalism and urban slang.',
  434: 'Someone forced to retract a confident statement must figuratively swallow the words they spoke. English has used “eat one’s words” for an embarrassing recantation since at least the 1500s.',
  435: '“Famous last words” originally referred literally to memorable statements made before death. During the 1900s it became a sarcastic reply to a confident prediction likely to prove wrong.',
  436: 'Pleasant music gives direct enjoyment to a listener. Comparing welcome news with “music to the ears” has been common for centuries and echoes older literary descriptions of sweet speech.',
  437: 'A deaf ear cannot receive sound, however clearly something is said. The phrase became a metaphor for warnings or requests that are deliberately ignored.',
  438: 'Information that enters one ear and immediately leaves through the other has not stayed in the mind. The humorous anatomy describes forgetting or failing to pay attention.',
  439: 'Ears represent listening, so saying “I’m all ears” exaggerates attention until the listener seems to consist entirely of ears. The expression became common in the 1800s.',
  440: 'Musicians who play by ear reproduce or improvise music without reading a score. The skill became a metaphor for proceeding without a fixed plan and adjusting as events unfold.',
  441: 'A person whose head and shoulders rise above a group is noticeably taller. By the 1800s, that visible superiority was also describing someone or something much better than competitors.',
  442: 'Two people leaning their heads together can speak privately and combine their thinking. The physical arrangement became a straightforward metaphor for collaboration.',
  443: '“Level” suggests balance and stability, while the head represents judgement. Keeping a level head therefore means remaining calm and sensible when emotion might disturb one’s balance.',
  444: 'Head and toe mark the upper and lower extremes of the human body. “From head to toe” has long meant covering the whole person completely.',
  445: 'Someone forced to respond while still standing or moving has no time for lengthy preparation. “Think on your feet” became a compliment for quick, adaptable judgement.',
  446: 'A choice that captures the advantages of two different situations avoids sacrificing either one. Versions of “the best of both worlds” became common during the 1800s.',
  447: 'Separate worlds suggest the greatest imaginable distance or difference. “Worlds apart” therefore describes people, ideas or conditions that are profoundly unlike one another.',
  448: 'Ancient rulers and religious figures were pictured with the world beneath their feet as a sign of power. Modern use often softens the image into having opportunity, success or admiration available.',
  449: 'A world is an enormous amount, so “a world of difference” magnifies an ordinary difference into something fundamental and unmistakable.',
  450: 'The end of the world is the ultimate imagined disaster. Saying a setback is “not the end of the world” deliberately reduces its importance and offers reassurance.',
  451: 'The proverb “two’s company, three’s a crowd” was established in the 1800s. It jokes that a comfortable pair can lose privacy or harmony when a third person joins.',
  452: 'Six and half of twelve are mathematically identical. “Six of one and half a dozen of the other” therefore means two alternatives amount to the same thing.',
  453: 'Three has long been treated as a complete or fortunate number in stories and folklore. “Third time lucky” expresses hope that two failures will be followed by success.',
  454: 'Baseball gives a batter three strikes before an out. American English turned the rule into a warning that a third failure or offence will end an opportunity.',
  455: 'The numbers mean 24 hours a day, seven days a week. The compressed form “24/7” spread through late-20th-century business and service advertising for constant availability.',
  456: 'A mould forces cast material into one repeated shape. Breaking it became a metaphor for rejecting an established pattern and creating something genuinely different.',
  457: 'Letters carved into stone are difficult to alter or erase. “Set in stone” therefore came to describe a rule, plan or decision treated as permanent.',
  458: 'A chip broken from a block usually retains the same material and appearance. The expression has compared a child with a similar parent since at least the 1600s.',
  459: 'A stone lying in a path can make a traveller trip. Biblical translations helped preserve “stumbling block” as a metaphor for an obstacle, temptation or cause of failure.',
  460: 'Children and builders assemble larger structures from simple blocks. During the 1900s, “building blocks” became a general term for basic components used to create something more complex.',
  461: 'Children’s puzzles reveal a picture when numbered dots are joined in order. “Connect the dots” became a metaphor for linking separate clues to understand a larger pattern.',
  462: 'A jigsaw puzzle becomes intelligible only when its separate pieces are assembled correctly. The activity supplied a metaphor for combining incomplete evidence into a whole explanation.',
  463: 'A well-made glove follows the hand’s shape very closely. “Fit like a glove” has described an exceptionally precise and comfortable fit since the 1700s.',
  464: 'A puzzle with one absent piece cannot form its intended complete picture. The image became a metaphor for the crucial fact, person or component still lacking.',
  465: 'A picture is complete when every intended detail or piece is present. The phrase also means supplying the final information needed to understand an entire situation.',
  466: 'A secure position in sunlight suggests warmth, recognition and prosperity. German statesman Bernhard von Bülow famously demanded a “place in the sun” for Germany in 1897, helping give the phrase political force.',
  467: 'Ecclesiastes repeatedly uses “under the sun” for life and activity in the human world. “Everything under the sun” expanded that biblical scope to mean virtually everything imaginable.',
  468: 'Stars are distant and apparently unreachable, making them natural symbols of the highest ambition. Advice to reach for them encourages aiming beyond ordinary limits.',
  469: 'Astrology teaches that the positions of stars can reveal or govern human destiny. Something “written in the stars” is therefore imagined as predetermined by fate.',
  470: 'People have credited favourable fate to a lucky star since ancient astrology. “Thank your lucky stars” expresses gratitude for unexpectedly good fortune.',
  471: 'A blow to the head or sudden dizziness can create flashing points of light in one’s vision. That physical effect produced the familiar image of “seeing stars.”',
  472: 'Shakespeare’s Romeo and Juliet calls its lovers “star-cross’d,” meaning their destinies are opposed by the stars. The phrase now describes a romance frustrated by fate.',
  473: 'The Moon is an extremely high and difficult target. “Shoot for the moon” became modern encouragement to attempt an ambitious goal even when success is uncertain.',
  474: 'A moon provides a natural measure of passing months. “Many moons ago” became a poetic way to mean long ago, although stereotyped claims assigning it to one particular Indigenous language should be treated cautiously.',
  475: 'The Moon is impossibly difficult to obtain. Asking for it therefore became a metaphor for making an extravagant or unrealistic demand.',
  476: 'Travelling around a circle returns someone repeatedly to the same places without progress. The shape became an obvious metaphor for confused activity that achieves nothing.',
  477: 'A complete circle returns to its starting point. “Come full circle” therefore describes a process, fashion or life returning to where it began.',
  478: 'Ancient geometers tried to construct a square equal in area to a circle using only a straightedge and compass. The task was eventually proved impossible, so “square the circle” means attempting the impossible.',
  479: '“Cut to the chase” came from the film industry, where editors could skip slower material and move directly to an action sequence. Literal examples appear by 1929, with the everyday figurative use spreading later.',
  480: 'An animal spinning after its own tail expends effort without getting anywhere. The comic image became a metaphor for a frustrating and self-defeating pursuit.',
  481: 'A vehicle may continue moving briefly even when its fuel gauge reads empty. “Running on empty” became a modern metaphor for continuing with almost no energy or resources left.',
  482: 'A person who lands already running loses no time before moving forward. The phrase became a metaphor for beginning a job or situation immediately and energetically; dramatic military origin stories are not necessary to explain it.',
  483: 'Steam powered engines lose force as their pressure and fuel are exhausted. “Running out of steam” became an industrial-age metaphor for losing energy, enthusiasm or momentum.',
  484: 'A race containing only one horse has no real competition and its outcome is effectively certain. The sporting image became a description of an uncontested contest.',
  485: 'Horses running side by side may have their necks level near the finish line. Racing supplied “neck and neck” for competitors whose positions are extremely close.',
  486: 'The proverb reminds people that wealth does not grow naturally like fruit and must instead be earned or managed. English versions about money and trees became common in the 1800s.',
  487: 'A miser seems to grip every penny so tightly that it is pinched between the fingers. “Penny-pincher” became a vivid name for someone extremely reluctant to spend money.',
  488: 'A fortune is a very large amount of wealth. Saying something “cost a fortune” is a straightforward exaggeration for an exceptionally high price.',
  489: 'A mint manufactures an enormous supply of coins, so it became shorthand for great wealth. Something “worth a mint” is imagined as valuable as the money-producing institution itself.',
  490: 'The phrase originally concerned arranging one’s income and expenses so their totals could meet without a gap. It became standard language for managing to live within available money.',
  491: 'In bookkeeping and commerce, the break-even point is where income equals costs, producing neither profit nor loss. The term spread into everyday calculations during the 1900s.',
  492: '“Pay through the nose” has meant paying an excessive price since at least the 1600s. Its underlying nose metaphor is unknown, and colourful stories about old taxes or punishments are unsupported.',
  493: 'In a ledger or restaurant account, the total is written at the foot or bottom of the bill. To “foot the bill” originally meant total it, then shifted to mean paying the amount.',
  494: 'A tab is a running account, especially at a bar or restaurant. The person who picks it up accepts responsibility for paying what has accumulated.',
  495: 'Burning money would be the ultimate display of having more wealth than one needs. “Money to burn” therefore means enough disposable money to spend freely or wastefully.',
  496: 'A card player who holds cards close to the chest prevents opponents reading the hand. The poker image became advice to keep plans or information private.',
  497: 'Success in card games depends on using the cards one has skilfully and at the right moment. “Play your cards right” broadened into making good use of circumstances and opportunities.',
  498: 'A secretly arranged deck can make one player far more likely to lose. “The cards are stacked against you” became a metaphor for circumstances deliberately or unfairly arranged to your disadvantage.',
  499: 'A wild card can represent another card or enter competition outside the normal qualification rules. Its flexibility made it a metaphor for an unpredictable person or factor.',
  500: 'Poker players try to conceal emotion so opponents cannot infer the strength of their cards. The unreadable expression became known as a “poker face” during the 1800s.',
}
